1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,330
Last time, we compared the frog and the mosquito.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:07,340
In both, the young look nothing like the adult, but the frog

3
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:10,580
has three stages, and the mosquito has four.

4
00:00:11,050 --> 00:00:13,300
Then Basil asked a tricky question!

5
00:00:16,097 --> 00:00:18,547
A little honeybee is hiding in this video.

6
00:00:18,807 --> 00:00:20,037
Can you spot it?

7
00:00:20,162 --> 00:00:22,112
Look for a fuzzy, striped body!

8
00:00:22,242 --> 00:00:24,082
Lock in first with your fingers.

9
00:00:24,352 --> 00:00:26,182
One finger for every point.

10
00:00:26,769 --> 00:00:27,919
Today you'll learn:

11
00:00:28,121 --> 00:00:29,761
No first or last stage.

12
00:00:29,965 --> 00:00:31,835
Same stages, still different.

13
00:00:32,031 --> 00:00:33,621
Why life cycles matter.

14
00:00:35,370 --> 00:00:36,250
Round one.

15
00:00:36,550 --> 00:00:37,800
Which came first?

16
00:00:38,090 --> 00:00:39,140
One, the egg.

17
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:40,540
Two, the chicken.

18
00:00:40,830 --> 00:00:43,370
Three, neither: it goes round and round.

19
00:00:43,660 --> 00:00:45,140
Show me with your fingers.

20
00:00:50,608 --> 00:00:51,588
Locked in!

21
00:00:51,716 --> 00:00:52,606
One, the egg!

22
00:00:52,776 --> 00:00:56,186
The egg is always the first word when we say the stages.

23
00:00:56,241 --> 00:00:56,691
Three.

24
00:00:57,271 --> 00:01:00,231
Every egg came from a hen, and every hen came from an

25
00:01:00,251 --> 00:01:00,751
egg.

26
00:01:00,961 --> 00:01:02,461
It just goes round.

27
00:01:02,517 --> 00:01:03,117
Three!

28
00:01:03,477 --> 00:01:06,617
A loop goes round and round, so nothing comes first.

29
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:07,930
Be an egg!

30
00:01:08,270 --> 00:01:09,260
Shrink down.

31
00:01:09,530 --> 00:01:10,470
Quietly.

32
00:01:12,751 --> 00:01:15,431
You are an egg, warm under the mother hen.

33
00:01:16,251 --> 00:01:17,471
Days go by.

34
00:01:18,281 --> 00:01:19,001
Crack!

35
00:01:19,831 --> 00:01:20,781
You hatch.

36
00:01:21,591 --> 00:01:23,081
You are a fluffy chick.

37
00:01:23,596 --> 00:01:26,486
You eat, and you grow, and your feathers grow.

38
00:01:27,266 --> 00:01:28,486
Weeks go by.

39
00:01:29,266 --> 00:01:30,736
You are an adult hen.

40
00:01:31,249 --> 00:01:33,319
One morning, you lay an egg of your own.

41
00:01:33,339 --> 00:01:37,589
It hatches into a chick, and round it goes again.

42
00:01:38,110 --> 00:01:39,470
So where did it start?

43
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:43,660
You joined in at the egg, but you could have joined anywhere.

44
00:01:45,310 --> 00:01:47,340
The answer is three: neither!

45
00:01:47,630 --> 00:01:48,700
Three fingers?

46
00:01:48,970 --> 00:01:50,190
One finger up!

47
00:01:50,836 --> 00:01:51,966
Look closer.

48
00:01:52,546 --> 00:01:56,306
Put your finger anywhere on the loop, and follow the arrows round.

49
00:01:56,886 --> 00:01:58,240
You always come back.

50
00:01:58,260 --> 00:01:59,930
It is like day and night!

51
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:01,270
Which comes first?

52
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:02,260
Neither.

53
00:02:02,410 --> 00:02:04,191
They just keep going round.

54
00:02:04,211 --> 00:02:07,521
The stages in a life cycle are repeated.

55
00:02:08,101 --> 00:02:10,691
So there is no first or last stage.

56
00:02:11,214 --> 00:02:13,854
Repeated means it happens again and again.

57
00:02:14,084 --> 00:02:17,124
So we never put numbers on the stages, because none of them

58
00:02:17,144 --> 00:02:18,174
is first!

59
00:02:20,875 --> 00:02:22,035
Say it with me!

60
00:02:22,743 --> 00:02:25,193
The stages in a life cycle are repeated.

61
00:02:25,813 --> 00:02:27,823
There is no first or last stage.

62
00:02:31,452 --> 00:02:32,352
Showdown!

63
00:02:32,662 --> 00:02:33,792
Double points.

64
00:02:34,062 --> 00:02:36,302
Momo's homework has one mistake.

65
00:02:36,582 --> 00:02:38,362
Line one, two, or three?

66
00:02:38,652 --> 00:02:40,122
Show me with your fingers.

67
00:02:40,184 --> 00:02:43,144
One: in both, the young do not look like the adult.

68
00:02:43,164 --> 00:02:47,014
Two: the mosquito larva lives near the water surface, but the beetle

69
00:02:47,034 --> 00:02:48,564
larva lives on land.

70
00:02:48,614 --> 00:02:52,434
Three: the mosquito has four stages, but the beetle has three.

71
00:02:58,046 --> 00:02:59,026
Locked in!

72
00:02:59,154 --> 00:02:59,894
Three!

73
00:02:59,964 --> 00:03:03,424
The mealworm turns into a pupa before it becomes a beetle.

74
00:03:03,634 --> 00:03:05,804
So the beetle has four stages too!

75
00:03:07,179 --> 00:03:08,269
Three fingers?

76
00:03:08,509 --> 00:03:10,439
Put up two more on your score hand!

77
00:03:10,789 --> 00:03:11,419
Oops!

78
00:03:11,709 --> 00:03:15,069
The beetle's pupa lies so still that I forgot to count it.

79
00:03:15,139 --> 00:03:16,829
So how are they different?

80
00:03:17,481 --> 00:03:18,631
Look closer.

81
00:03:19,211 --> 00:03:24,271
The stages are the same, so now we compare their physical features.

82
00:03:24,881 --> 00:03:27,291
Physical features are the parts we can see.

83
00:03:27,461 --> 00:03:30,241
The wriggler is thin, and hangs at the water surface.

84
00:03:30,361 --> 00:03:33,531
The mealworm is golden and hard, and crawls on land!

85
00:03:36,230 --> 00:03:37,460
Say it with me!

86
00:03:38,174 --> 00:03:42,954
Observable characteristics include the number of stages and the physical features of

87
00:03:42,974 --> 00:03:43,994
the animals.

88
00:03:46,615 --> 00:03:48,415
Why do life cycles matter?

89
00:03:48,695 --> 00:03:49,375
Look!

90
00:03:49,665 --> 00:03:53,085
The butterfly from last time is laying an egg on the lime

91
00:03:53,105 --> 00:03:53,759
plant.

92
00:03:53,779 --> 00:03:58,129
Life cycles ensure that living things of the same kind can continue

93
00:03:58,149 --> 00:03:59,259
to live on Earth.

94
00:03:59,781 --> 00:04:03,331
With too few lime plants, there would be fewer larvae, fewer pupae

95
00:04:03,351 --> 00:04:04,861
and fewer butterflies.

96
00:04:04,911 --> 00:04:07,591
So we plant more, and the cycle keeps going!

97
00:04:08,657 --> 00:04:09,762
Say it with us!

98
00:04:10,422 --> 00:04:11,672
Same or different?

99
00:04:12,002 --> 00:04:13,302
Compare them both!

100
00:04:16,822 --> 00:04:18,151
Count the stages!

101
00:04:18,402 --> 00:04:19,535
Three or four!

102
00:04:23,222 --> 00:04:24,445
Round and round!

103
00:04:24,802 --> 00:04:26,202
No first, no last!

104
00:04:30,377 --> 00:04:31,847
Show me your score hand!

105
00:04:32,127 --> 00:04:33,177
More than one?

106
00:04:33,477 --> 00:04:34,837
You beat the Buddies.

107
00:04:35,137 --> 00:04:36,197
Three fingers?

108
00:04:36,467 --> 00:04:37,477
High five!

109
00:04:37,982 --> 00:04:39,452
Did you spot the honeybee?

110
00:04:39,732 --> 00:04:41,922
It was on a flower of the lime plant!

111
00:04:42,057 --> 00:04:43,657
Here is a fun fact!

112
00:04:43,807 --> 00:04:46,807
A queen honeybee can lay more than a thousand eggs in a

113
00:04:46,827 --> 00:04:47,857
single day.

114
00:04:48,107 --> 00:04:50,527
That keeps the bees' life cycle going!

115
00:04:50,651 --> 00:04:51,931
Tell someone at home!

116
00:04:52,720 --> 00:04:56,380
Next time: a magnet, a steel pin and a copper wire!

117
00:04:56,650 --> 00:04:58,910
The pin and the wire are both metal.

118
00:04:59,210 --> 00:05:00,870
Which one will the magnet pull?

119
00:05:01,150 --> 00:05:02,540
One, the steel pin.

120
00:05:02,830 --> 00:05:04,380
Two, the copper wire.

121
00:05:04,690 --> 00:05:05,710
Three, both.

122
00:05:05,970 --> 00:05:07,300
Lock in your answer!

123
00:05:07,571 --> 00:05:09,771
Let's read today's key words together!

124
00:05:10,061 --> 00:05:10,821
Ready?

125
00:05:11,108 --> 00:05:12,018
Repeated.

126
00:05:13,434 --> 00:05:15,234
No first or last stage.

127
00:05:17,445 --> 00:05:18,865
Physical features.

128
00:05:20,658 --> 00:05:21,598
Well done!

129
00:05:22,580 --> 00:05:23,681
Say it with us!

130
00:05:24,481 --> 00:05:25,231
Ready?

131
00:05:25,355 --> 00:05:26,015
One!

132
00:05:26,355 --> 00:05:27,055
two!

133
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:28,535
Now you know!
